      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bulgarian railroad workers named Plevna after the Bulgarian city of Pleven, echoing the 1877 Siege of Plevna.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plevna%2C_Montana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Milwaukee Road tracks that founded Plevna were abandoned in 1980, but BNSF still runs freight through the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://roamingmontana.com/plevna/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Plevna sits at an elevation of 2,809 feet above sea level in Montana.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/128461"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Plevna is the only community in Montana with a Bulgarian name, a rare cultural footnote in the state.",
